A continuous basis assigns to each element a directed family of way-below approximants whose supremum recovers the element.

**Definition (`ContinuousBasis`).** A map sending each $x$ in a complete lattice to a set $\mathrm{basis}(x)$ is a continuous basis when, for every $x$:
- $\mathrm{basis}(x)$ is directed,
- every $y\in\mathrm{basis}(x)$ satisfies $y\ll x$, and
- $x=\bigsqcup\mathrm{basis}(x)$.

*Proof.* These three clauses are the definition of a continuous basis. ∎
